@flexiarg apparatus/every-wait-has-a-deadline
@title Every Wait Has A Deadline
@keywords deadline, timeout, hang, awaiting, absolute-time, expiry
@audience apparatus builders, futon developers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status promoted (field trial 2026-09-08, APM repair program S1-S10; evidence: futon3c/holes/technotes/TN-apm-principle-scoring-ledger.md @ b0c36489; Joe's ruling 2026-09-08)
@references [apparatus/loudness-is-conserved apparatus/monitors-measure-the-work]
@see-also apparatus/success-must-not-resemble-failure
@why APM: `:awaiting-substrate` had no upper bound, so a wait was indistinguishable from a hang — T-apm-recurring-failure-end-to-end.md argues the quota fix and the f193 stall are the same defect seen a day apart. The park-deadline footgun (futon3c README-park.md) is the twin from the other side: a relative duration read as absolute epoch-ms fires instantly.
@how own mechanism: every wait state carries a verifiably absolute expiry, and expiry routes to a decision (retry, escalate, alarm), never back into the same wait.
@violation-signature A wait state with no expiry field; a deadline expressed in relative units where absolute is required (or vice versa); a component whose "waiting" and "hung" states produce identical observations. Trial amendment (2026-09-08, S7): a deadline whose value the waiting party can set arbitrarily far ahead is NOT a bound — the APM queue's wait carried a deadline, but the watchdog read the mover's own `:not-before-ms` field AS that deadline, so a far-future wake could never be late. Bound the direction that hurts; the fix also showed the danger is asymmetric (a past wake merely retries immediately, and refusing overdue wakes after a long stop turns restart into a park storm).
! conclusion: A wait that cannot expire is indistinguishable from a hang; every wait carries a verifiably absolute deadline whose expiry routes to a decision.

  + context: An apparatus waits on things it does not control: a provider quota to lift, a substrate write to land, a reply to arrive.

  + IF:
    A component enters a state whose exit depends on an external event.

  + HOWEVER:
    The wait was added as a remedy (usually for a burn — see apparatus/loudness-is-conserved), and bounding it feels like re-introducing the failure it fixed; so it ships unbounded, and the stall detector then has nothing to distinguish patience from death.

  + THEN:
    Give every wait a deadline. Make the deadline verifiably absolute — an already-past deadline fires instantly, which is a fault wearing this principle's clothes — and route expiry to an explicit decision point, never silently back into the wait.

  + BECAUSE:
    Detectors can only work over bounded waits: an unbounded wait defeats the watchdog by definition, since no elapsed time is evidence of anything.

    + evidence: APM 2026-09-06: 62 frames parked in one hour against a refusing provider, then the remedy created a wait with no upper bound; 2026-09-07: f193 sat in an unresolvable state for an hour with the coordinator ticking normally.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Grep the apparatus for wait/awaiting/hold states; each needs an expiry and an expiry route.
    - Test each deadline's clock basis by writing one that has already passed and confirming the instant-fire is caught.
